<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="diphthamide biosynthesis II (eukaryotes)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7546" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: DIPHTHAMIDE &quot;Diphthamide&quot;| is a unique posttranslationally modified histidine residue found only in translation elongation factor 2 (|FRAME: Elongation-factor-2|). This factor is found in archaea and in all eukaryotes, but not in eubacteria, and is conserved from archaea to humans. The role of diphthamide is still not understood. Mutant defective in its biosynthesis are viable in yeast, but mutant mice are retarded in growth and development, and almost always die before birth |CITS:[16648478][18765564]|. A potential role of diphthamide may be to protect ribosomes fom ribosome-inactivating proteins (such as ricin), which are widely distributed in nature |CITS: [18460012]|. The conversion of |FRAME: HIS | to |FRAME: CPD-17005| is a complex process. It starts by the transfer of a 3-amino-3-carboxypropyl moiety from |FRAME: S-ADENOSYLMETHIONINE|. Four different genes of |FRAME: TAX-4932| have been shown to be required for this step in eukaryotes. Three of these genes, |FRAME: G-11954|, |FRAME: G-11955| and |FRAME: G-11956|, are believed to form a |FRAME: CPLX-8046| complex |CITS: [8406038][15485916]|. The fourth gene, |FRAME: G3O-31725|, is similar to DnaJ-type chaperones, and is assumed to be responsible for the proper folding of one or more of the other DPH proteins |CITS: [15485916]|. The next step, catalyzed by the |FRAME: G-11958|-encoded |FRAME: MONOMER-15582|, is the transfer of four methyl groups, donated by four molecules of |FRAME: S-ADENOSYLMETHIONINE|, to form a |FRAME: Diphthine-methyl-ester-EF2 &quot;diphtine methyl ester residue&quot;| |CITS: [1508200][24739148]|. This is followed by the action of |FRAME: G-34532|, a methylesterase that removes the methyl group connected to the oxygen, leaving a |FRAME: DIPHTINE diphtine| residue |CITS: [24739148]|. The last step is the amidation of |FRAME: DIPHTINE &quot;diphtine&quot;| to |FRAME: DIPHTHAMIDE &quot;diphthamide&quot;|, catalyzed by the |FRAME: G-15503|-encoded |FRAME: MONOMER-17855| |CITS: [23169644]|. Diphthamide serves as the target for two important bacterial toxins - the |FRAME: CPLX-8048| (DT) and the Pseudomonas |FRAME: MONOMER-15589 &quot;endotoxin A&quot;|. Both toxins catalyze the transfer of |FRAME: ADENOSINE_DIPHOSPHATE_RIBOSE| from NAD+ to |FRAME: DIPHTHAMIDE| on eEF-2, inactivating it and halting cellular protein synthesis, causing cell death |CITS: [15485916]|. By targeting this unique modified amino acid which does not exist in bacteria, these pathogens are able to shut down the eukaryotic protein synthesis machinery without jeopardizing their own system.</Description>
